.TH "st\-server" "1" "Jun 2026" "Open source version of the STMicroelectronics STLINK Tools" "stlink"
.SH NAME
st\-server \- Serve a local ST\-LINK over TCP for remote stlink tools
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[I]st\-server\f[R] [\f[I]OPTIONS\f[R]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\f[I]st\-server\f[R] opens one locally attached ST\-LINK probe and
exposes the stlink backend operations over TCP.
A remote \f[I]st\-flash\f[R](1), \f[I]st\-info\f[R](1), or
\f[I]st\-util\f[R](1) client can then use \f[I]\(enremote\f[R] to drive
the probe from another machine.
.PP
The server owns the USB connection to the ST\-LINK.
The client runs the normal stlink target logic such as target
connection, chip detection, erase, program, verify, and GDB server
behavior.
.PP
By default \f[I]st\-server\f[R] listens only on the loopback address
\f[I]127.0.0.1:4500\f[R].
Use \f[I]\(enbind\f[R] to listen on another IPv4 address.
Binding to \f[I]0.0.0.0\f[R] or using \f[I]\(enbind=:PORT\f[R] exposes
the probe to the network; only do this on trusted networks or behind
another access\-control layer such as SSH.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\-\-bind \f[I]ADDR\f[R][:\f[I]PORT\f[R]]
IPv4 address and optional port to listen on.
The default is \f[I]127.0.0.1:4500\f[R].
Use \f[I]0.0.0.0\f[R] to listen on all IPv4 interfaces.
The shorthand \f[I]:4500\f[R] is accepted and means
\f[I]0.0.0.0:4500\f[R].
.TP
\-\-port \f[I]PORT\f[R]
TCP port to listen on.
This is kept for compatibility and can be combined with
\f[I]\(enbind\f[R] when the bind address does not include a port.
.TP
\-\-serial \f[I]iSerial\f[R]
Serial number of the local ST\-LINK device to serve.
To serve multiple probes concurrently, run one \f[I]st\-server\f[R]
process per probe on different ports.
.TP
\-\-freq \f[I]kHz\f[R]
SWD/JTAG frequency in kHz used by the server when opening the local
probe.
Client commands may still set their own frequency during the remote
connect sequence.
.TP
\-\-verbose[=\f[I]LEVEL\f[R]]
Enable verbose logging.
If \f[I]LEVEL\f[R] is omitted, debug logging is enabled.
.TP
\-\-help
Print usage information.
.SH EXAMPLES
Serve the first local ST\-LINK on the default loopback address:
.IP
.EX
$ st\-server
.EE
.PP
Serve a specific probe on all IPv4 interfaces:
.IP
.EX
$ st\-server \-\-serial 57FF72067265575742132067 \-\-bind=:4500
.EE
.PP
Use an SSH tunnel to keep \f[I]st\-server\f[R] bound to loopback.
On the host with the probe, run:
.IP
.EX
$ st\-server
.EE
.PP
On the laptop, forward the port and use the tunnel:
.IP
.EX
$ ssh \-N \-L 4500:127.0.0.1:4500 pi\(atraspberrypi &
$ st\-flash \-\-remote 127.0.0.1:4500 write firmware.bin 0x08000000
$ st\-info  \-\-probe \-\-remote 127.0.0.1:4500
$ st\-util  \-\-remote 127.0.0.1:4500
.EE
.PP
Serve two probes concurrently (one \f[I]st\-server\f[R] per probe, on
different ports):
.IP
.EX
$ st\-server \-\-serial aaa \-\-port 4500
$ st\-server \-\-serial bbb \-\-port 4501
.EE
.SH SECURITY
The protocol has no authentication or encryption: a client that can
reach the port has full debug and flash control of the probe.
The default loopback bind (\f[I]127.0.0.1\f[R]) avoids exposing the
probe to the network.
Use an SSH tunnel for remote access, or use \f[I]\(enbind=0.0.0.0\f[R]
only on a trusted, isolated network.
.SH RECOVERY
If \f[I]st\-server\f[R] is killed during a flash operation, normal
\f[I]SIGINT\f[R] and \f[I]SIGTERM\f[R] shutdown paths close the ST\-LINK
handle cleanly.
A forced kill, process crash, or USB firmware fault can still leave the
ST\-LINK unresponsive.
If local tools such as \f[I]st\-info \(enprobe\f[R] also hang, reset the
USB device with a tool such as \f[I]usbreset\f[R] or physically replug
the probe.
